class Solution {
  public int[] cableFamiliesPositiveProofStrip(int[][] links, int k) {
    int[][] a=links; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int n=a.length;int[]parent=new int[n],size=new int[n];for(int v=0;v<n;v++){parent[v]=v;size[v]=1;}class DSU {int find(int x){while(parent[x]!=x){parent[x]=parent[parent[x]];x=parent[x];}return x;}}DSU d=new DSU();for(int u=0;u<n;u++)for(int v=u+1;v<n;v++)if(a[u][v]!=0){int x=d.find(u),y=d.find(v);if(x!=y){if(size[x]<size[y]){int t=x;x=y;y=t;}parent[y]=x;size[x]+=size[y];}}for(int v=0;v<n;v++)out.add(size[d.find(v)]);}
    return out.stream().filter(x->x>0).mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray();
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build the result list

This graph has two-way links between points. Points belong to the same group if you can travel between them, even through other points. Use union-find: start with one group per point, then join groups when a link connects them. For each point, count all points in its group, including itself. Record these counts in point order. A link from a point to itself does not make a larger group. A graph with no points gives an empty result list.

Return the answer

Return only numbers greater than 0 from the result list. Keep their order and any repeated numbers. If none matches, return [].

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(V^2 alpha(V)); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length; V is the number of graph points. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

